  "agent/requirements.md": "{{{generatedMarker}}}\n\n# Requirements\n\nRead this before adding a capability, and before extending anything that looks like it already does what you were asked for.\n\n- A requirement is **prescriptive**: it states what should be true, never what happened. `status: retired` means the capability was BUILT and deliberately REMOVED — that entry is a prohibition, not history, and the right response to it is to stop, not to rebuild.\n- The **node index** below answers *\"does anything claim the thing I am about to change?\"*. Match the literal FQN.\n- **The ledger under it is not the same list.** A retired capability carries no `@implementedBy` — there is nothing left to point at — so it appears there and in no index. Read it before you conclude a capability does not exist yet.\n\n## Node index\n\n{{#hasNodeIndex}}\n| Node | Kind | Claimed by |\n|---|---|---|\n{{#nodeIndex}}\n| `{{{address}}}` | `{{{concern}}}` | {{{claims}}} |\n{{/nodeIndex}}\n{{#hasStatements}}\n\n### What those requirements say\n\n{{#statements}}\n- `{{{path}}}` (`{{{subType}}}`) — {{{statement}}}\n{{/statements}}\n{{/hasStatements}}\n{{/hasNodeIndex}}\n{{^hasNodeIndex}}\nNo requirement resolves to a model node. The common reason is the ordinary one: an entry at L4 or L5 that simply declares no `@implementedBy` yet, which is legal and is what a `planned` entry looks like. The others are an entry sitting ABOVE the L4 link floor (where a claim is not permitted), a `retired` entry (where it is forbidden outright), and a claim that names a node nothing resolves — `meta verify` is what says which.\n{{/hasNodeIndex}}\n\n## The ledger\n\n{{{ledger}}}\n",
  "agent/schema.md": "{{{generatedMarker}}}\n\n# Schema\n\n{{#physical}}\nThe physical shape of the `{{{dialect}}}` database this model generates. Read it before writing a query, a migration, or anything that names a table or a column.\n{{/physical}}\n{{^physical}}\nThe tables, columns and keys this model declares. Read it before writing a query, a migration, or anything that names a table or a column. SQL types, defaults, indexes, checks, constraint names and referential actions are not on this page: the migration files carry them.\n{{/physical}}\n\n- The **DDL is not repeated here.** The migration files are the DDL, they are generated, and they are what runs — this page describes the schema they produce.\n- Change the schema by changing the **metadata** and running `meta migrate`. Never hand-apply SQL to a live database: it drifts from the migration history and collides at the next migrate.\n- `Field` and `Declared` are the METADATA names. Edit those; the column name follows.\n\n{{#descriptionNudge}}\n> **Nothing in this model declares a `description`.** The tables and columns below are named but not explained. Adding `description` to an entity or a field is the single highest-value change to this page: it is what tells a reader *which* of two plausible columns to use, and it is the same text `meta migrate` emits as a `COMMENT ON`, so it lands in the database too.\n\n{{/descriptionNudge}}\n{{#hasTables}}\n## Tables\n\n{{#tables}}\n### {{{heading}}}\n\n{{#tableDeclaredBy}}\nDeclared by `{{{.}}}`.\n{{/tableDeclaredBy}}\n{{#tableDescription}}\n\n> {{{.}}}\n{{/tableDescription}}\n\n{{#physical}}\n| Column | Field | Declared | SQL type | Null | Default | Key |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n{{/physical}}\n{{^physical}}\n| Column | Field | Declared | Null | Key |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n{{/physical}}\n{{#columns}}\n| `{{{name}}}` | {{{fieldCell}}} | {{{declaredCell}}} |{{#physical}} {{{sqlTypeCell}}} |{{/physical}} {{{nullCell}}} |{{#physical}} {{{defaultCell}}} |{{/physical}} {{{keyCell}}} |\n{{/columns}}\n{{#hasDescribed}}\n\n{{#described}}\n- `{{{name}}}` — {{{text}}}\n{{/described}}\n{{/hasDescribed}}\n{{#hasIndexes}}\n\n**Indexes**\n\n{{#indexes}}\n- {{{.}}}\n{{/indexes}}\n{{/hasIndexes}}\n{{#hasForeignKeys}}\n\n**Foreign keys**\n\n{{#foreignKeys}}\n- {{{.}}}\n{{/foreignKeys}}\n{{/hasForeignKeys}}\n{{#hasChecks}}\n\n**Checks**\n\n{{#checks}}\n- {{{.}}}\n{{/checks}}\n{{/hasChecks}}\n\n{{/tables}}\n{{/hasTables}}\n{{#hasViews}}\n## Views\n\nA view is generated from its projection's `origin.*` children{{#hasReportView}} or its report's dimensions and measures{{/hasReportView}} — it is derived, never hand-written. Editing the view SQL directly is drift the tool cannot see.\n\n{{#views}}\n### {{{heading}}}\n\n{{#viewDeclaredBy}}\nDeclared by `{{{.}}}`.\n{{/viewDeclaredBy}}\n{{#hasLineage}}\n\n| Column | Lineage |\n|---|---|\n{{#lineage}}\n| `{{{column}}}` | {{{text}}} |\n{{/lineage}}\n{{/hasLineage}}\n\n{{/views}}\n{{/hasViews}}\n{{#hasRelationships}}\n## Relationships\n\n{{#relationships}}\n- {{{.}}}\n{{/relationships}}\n\n{{/hasRelationships}}\n{{#hasEnums}}\n## Enums\n\nMembers are the values the wire and the generated types use.{{#physical}} Whether the DATABASE also refuses a value outside the set is that table's **Checks** above — an `@isArray` enum carries none, and neither does a view column.{{/physical}}\n\n| Field | Members | Storage |\n|---|---|---|\n{{#enums}}\n| `{{{field}}}` | {{{members}}} | {{{storage}}} |\n{{/enums}}\n{{/hasEnums}}\n",
